The reason your AppArmor profile is not capturing touch and rm is because your profile is incomplete.

Your script:

#!/bin/bash
touch /tmp/file.txt
echo "New File Created"

rm -rf /tmp/file.txt
echo "New file removed"

executes these binaries:

/usr/bin/touch

/usr/bin/rm

/usr/bin/echo (built into bash)


But your profile only contains:

/home/armor/app.sh r,
/usr/bin/bash ix,
/usr/bin/gnurm mr,

There is no rule for touch or rm, and you also have a typo:

/usr/bin/gnurm mr,

There is no binary called gnurm. It should be:

/usr/bin/rm mrix,

Correct profile

abi <abi/5.0>,

include <tunables/global>

/home/armor/app.sh {
    include <abstractions/base>
    include <abstractions/bash>
    include <abstractions/consoles>
    include <abstractions/user-tmp>

    /home/armor/app.sh r,

    /usr/bin/bash ix,
    /usr/bin/touch mrix,
    /usr/bin/rm mrix,

    owner /etc/ld.so.cache r,
    owner /etc/locale.alias r,
}

Notice these additions:

include <abstractions/user-tmp>

/usr/bin/touch mrix,
/usr/bin/rm mrix,

The user-tmp abstraction provides the permissions required to access files under /tmp.


---

Reload the profile

sudo apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/home.armor.app.sh

or

sudo systemctl reload apparmor


---

Put it into enforce mode

sudo aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/home.armor.app.sh

Verify:

sudo aa-status


---

Run the script

/home/armor/app.sh

If you remove the following rules:

/usr/bin/touch mrix,
/usr/bin/rm mrix,

and reload the profile, AppArmor should generate DENIED audit messages.

Check them with:

sudo dmesg | grep DENIED

or

sudo journalctl -k | grep DENIED


---

One more thing

Your profile is generated with:

abi <abi/5.0>

while the example uses:

abi <abi/3.0>

This is not a problem—Ubuntu 24.04/25.04 uses ABI 5.0. The important issue is the missing touch, rm, and user-tmp rules.

If your goal is to learn AppArmor profiling, I can also show you how to use aa-genprof so that running app.sh automatically records every command (touch, rm, cat, mkdir, etc.) and builds the profile interactively.
